      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-71221",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point",
        "problem": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$",
        "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it.",
